{"term":"Dom","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"dominance","concept":"bdsm_dominant","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Abbreviation for 'dominant,' borrowed unchanged from English BDSM vocabulary; refers to a person who takes the dominant, controlling role in consensual BDSM power exchange.","english_term":"dom","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Identical to the English abbreviation; in German 'Dom' is also the standard word for cathedral (der Dom), creating potential context confusion; BDSM meaning is always disambiguated by context."}
{"term":"Switch","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"switch_role","concept":"bdsm_switch","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"English loanword used unchanged in German BDSM communities for a person who comfortably takes both dominant and submissive roles, either across different partners or within the same scene.","english_term":"switch","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"No native German equivalent is commonly used for this concept; the English term was adopted wholesale; recognition is substantially confined to BDSM-aware adults."}
{"term":"Unterwerfung","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"submission","concept":"bdsm_submission","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":20,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":86,"recognition_pct_peak":86,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Native German noun meaning 'subjugation' or 'submission'; in BDSM contexts refers to the act of a submissive yielding authority and control to a dominant partner within a power-exchange dynamic.","english_term":"submission / subjugation","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"A standard German word with broad non-sexual applications (political subjugation, religious submission, military surrender); its BDSM use specializes the general meaning. Emphasizes the discrete act of submitting more than the ongoing disposition, distinguishing it from 'Unterwürfigkeit.'"}
{"term":"Unterwürfigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"submission","concept":"bdsm_submission","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":15,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":74,"recognition_pct_peak":74,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Native German noun meaning 'submissiveness' or 'obsequiousness'; in BDSM contexts describes the dispositional quality of a submissive — an ingrained tendency toward deference, compliance, and yielding to dominant authority.","english_term":"submissiveness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"In everyday German 'Unterwürfigkeit' carries a mildly pejorative connotation of servile obsequiousness; in BDSM it is reframed positively as a valued submissive trait. Distinguishes dispositional character from the act itself ('Unterwerfung')."}
{"term":"Rollenspiel","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"roleplay","concept":"bdsm_play","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"German compound noun for 'role play' (Rolle = role, Spiel = play/game); in BDSM and sexual contexts refers to erotic scenarios in which participants adopt characters, personas, or power roles for mutual arousal and fantasy.","english_term":"roleplay","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Universally known in German from non-sexual contexts (RPGs, therapy, education, theater); its sexual and BDSM meaning is widely understood from media exposure; recognition figures reflect the general word while sexual meaning is recognized by approximately 70–80% of adults."}
{"term":"Fesselung","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"bondage","concept":"bondage_restraint","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":35,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"German noun for the act of binding or physically restraining someone; derived from 'fesseln' (to tie, to restrain); the principal native German term for restraint or bondage in BDSM contexts.","english_term":"restraint / bondage","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Fesselung' is the main native German alternative to the English loanword 'Bondage'; also used non-sexually for police restraint and, figuratively, for anything that captivates or entraps; BDSM use is the dominant sexual meaning."}
{"term":"Handfesselung","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"wrist_bondage","concept":"bondage_restraint","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":35,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"German compound noun for wrist or hand restraint (Hand = hand, Fesselung = restraint); in BDSM contexts specifically the binding of a partner's hands or wrists as a form of erotic restraint.","english_term":"wrist bondage / hand restraint","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More specific than either 'Fesselung' (general restraint) or 'Bondage' (general practice); also used neutrally in police and security contexts for handcuffing; no single English term carries both the specificity and the BDSM register simultaneously."}
{"term":"Seilbindage","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"rope_bondage","concept":"bondage","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":35,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":28,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"German-French hybrid compound for rope bondage (Seil = rope, Bindage = bondage, from French); used in German BDSM communities for erotic restraint using rope specifically.","english_term":"rope bondage","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"A hybrid coinage blending native German 'Seil' with the semi-Francophone 'Bindage'; less common in community usage than 'Shibari' or 'Strick-Bondage'; represents an attempt to create a distinct German-language rope-bondage label. Niche even within the BDSM community."}
{"term":"Shibari","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"rope_bondage","concept":"shibari","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":30,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":42,"recognition_pct_peak":42,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Japanese loanword used identically in German BDSM communities for the Japanese rope bondage art form, emphasizing the aesthetic, technical, and artistic dimensions of rope restraint over purely functional binding.","english_term":"shibari","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Adopted from Japanese via the international BDSM community with no modification; carries stronger artistic and aesthetic connotations than 'Bondage' or 'Fesselung'; practitioners often distinguish Shibari as an art form from purely functional rope bondage."}
{"term":"Strick-Bondage","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"rope_bondage","concept":"bondage","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":38,"technical":18,"recognition_pct_current":33,"recognition_pct_peak":33,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"German-English hybrid compound for rope bondage (Strick = rope/cord, Bondage = English loanword); refers to erotic restraint using rope or cord, generally interchangeable with 'Seilbindage' in BDSM community usage.","english_term":"rope bondage","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Strick' typically denotes a coarser or heavier cord than 'Seil'; 'Strick' also colloquially means a rascal or scoundrel in German ('ein rechter Strick'); the hybrid formation combines native and borrowed vocabulary and is somewhat less artistically inflected than 'Shibari.'"}
{"term":"Knebel","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"gag_bdsm","concept":"bondage_restraint","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":45,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"German noun for a gag — any device placed in or over the mouth to muffle sound or enforce silence; in BDSM contexts used to restrict a submissive's ability to speak as a form of restraint and power exchange.","english_term":"gag","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Knebel' has a well-established political metaphor in German (gagging the press, a punishing contract: 'Knebelvertrag'); the BDSM sense is recognized community vocabulary. English 'gag' does not capture the word's breadth, as 'Knebel' can denote any mouth-restraint type without specifying ball gag, bit gag, etc."}
{"term":"Augenbinde","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"blindfold_sensory_deprivation","concept":"sensation_play","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":91,"recognition_pct_peak":91,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"German compound noun for blindfold (Augen = eyes, Binde = bandage/wrap); in BDSM contexts a blindfold used for sensory deprivation to heighten other sensations and reinforce the submissive's vulnerability and dependence on the dominant.","english_term":"blindfold","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The everyday German word for blindfold, also used in the children's game Blindekuh (blind man's bluff) and classical depictions of Justitia; its BDSM application is a specific use of the general object. Recognition of the word is high; recognition in its BDSM sense is approximately 50–60% of adults."}
